
---
import Navigation from "../../components/navigation.astro";
import { posts } from "../../data/posts.js";

const post = posts.find((post) => post.slug === "post_fourteen");
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
    <title>{post.title} | Ivy Duncan</title>
    <meta name="description" content={post.excerpt} />
  </head>

  <body>
    <Navigation />

    <main class="post-page">
      <article class="post-container">
        <header class="post-header">
          <a href="/blog" class="back-link">← Back to Blog</a>

          <p class="post-date">
            <time datetime={post.dateTime}>{post.date}</time>
          </p>

          <h1>{post.title}</h1>

          <div class="post-tags">
            {post.tags.map((tag) => (
              <a
                href={`/blog/tag/${tag.toLowerCase().replace(/\s+/g, "-")}`}
                class="post-tag"
              >
                {tag}
              </a>
            ))}
          </div>
        </header>

        <div class="post-content">
         <p>
            I had a lazy writing week this week, so insead of a boring Friday writing update, I thought I'd share some of my writing process. 
         </p>
          <p>
            Now, I want to preface this by saying that this is how I write my
            books, and it’s not necessarily how you should write yours. I think
            something different works for everyone, but I know when I was
            trying to write my first (and struggling) I did a lot of reading up
            on how other authors approached writing their novels.
          </p>

          <p>
            I feel like the first novel is the most important. Now, don’t get
            me wrong, I think my first novel I ever finished was shit. Like,
            absolutely terrible. But it was the hardest one to write, and
            finishing it taught me that I was capable of doing it.
          </p>

          <p>
            My writing process looks a little different since starting to
            publish—there are way more editing steps to go through—but I’m not
            gonna talk about that today.
          </p>

          <p>
            This post is to talk about the biggest hurdle: the first draft.
          </p>

          <p>
            I’d say there are four steps in constructing this draft for me, and
            the first one is super duper messy and actually quite anxiety
            inducing.
          </p>

          <h2>Step One: The Ideas Phase</h2>

          <p>
            I’ll call it “the ideas phase”. For me, this usually happens when
            I’m lying in bed trying (and failing) to sleep at night. The plots
            usually come to me then, and I open a document on my phone and
            start making a long (sometimes very very long) bulleted list with
            all the plot points that come to mind. Sometimes these are in
            order. Sometimes they’re not.
          </p>

          <p>
            I’ll use <em>Right Next Door</em> as an example. Here is the
            beginning of the bulleted list I made for <em>Right Next Door</em>:
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>
              When they’re together people keep hitting on Miles. (“Am I the
              ugly one on this relationship?”)
            </li>
            <li>Listen to music in bed together</li>
            <li>
              This is my brunette in glasses (wears them a lot but not all the
              time) who's cute as shit and dresses really well. Also, the two
              mains hate each other
            </li>
            <li>
              B has a job interview or something the next morning and has to
              leave suddenly and other two friends go talk to T
            </li>
            <li>T and B are left together and T kisses him and asks him out</li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            Notice that a lot of this didn’t end up in the final draft. Also,
            notice that it does not start at the beginning of the story.
          </p>

          <h2>Step Two: Organizing the Ideas</h2>

          <p>
            That’s where step two starts! At this point, once I have enough
            ideas written down, I create another document to organize these
            ideas and get them in order. For <em>Right Next Door</em>, this
            document is actually called “Be More Organized, Bitch”. It is in
            this phase that a lot of the ideas get cut out, because I realize
            they just don’t fit.
          </p>

          <p>For example, here is the beginning of that document:</p>

          <ul>
            <li>
              Beginning is that Ren got a text from Hale telling him to go say
              hi to the new neighbors, says he met one in the elevator and he
              seems kind of cute and Ren goes over there to say hello and a
              freshman boy opens the door and he tries to be friendly but the
              kid is a douche and they don’t get along
            </li>
            <li>
              It is Hale and Ren’s senior year and Miles and Logan’s junior year
            </li>
            <li>
              Ren is the type of person who goes on lots of dates but he’s
              rarely serious
            </li>
            <li>
              Hale and Logan take Ren and Miles out to dinner to tell them that
              they’re dating. Ren feels betrayed
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            This should sound a little more familiar if you’ve read the book.
          </p>

          <h2>Step Three: Dividing Everything into Chapters</h2>

          <p>
            Once this is all in order and sorted out, I move on to step three,
            which is dividing all these bullet points up into chapters, and
            making a document for each of them. This is probably my favorite
            step of the whole book writing process, since it’s like writing a
            book and thinking through all the fun parts without having to put
            in all the work to fill in the in between stuff.
          </p>

          <p>
            Here is the document for chapter one of <em>Right Next Door</em> at this step:
          </p>

          <div class="example-document">
            <p class="example-title">Part one- Chapter One</p>
            <ul>
              <li>
                Ren is unpacking when Hale tells him to go meet the neighbors.
                He and Hale moved in about a week ago, and while Hale’s room is
                all set up perfectly, Ren waited to unpack until the last minute
              </li>
              <li>
                Hale says he met one in the elevator and he seems kind of cute
                and Ren goes over there to say hello and a freshman boy opens
                the door (red hair, hazel eyes) and he tries to be friendly but
                the kid is a douche and they don’t get along and Ren is like
                “what’s cute about that”.
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <p>
            Notice this is the step where I start including quotes or exact
            phrases I want the characters to say.
          </p>

          <h2>Step Four: Writing the Actual Chapters</h2>

          <p>
            This is what I reference as I move on to step four, which is
            writing the actual chapters, which leads me to the elusive first
            draft.
          </p>

          <p>
            Doing it this way—with these four steps—has been a really good way
            for me to keep plots organized, and make sure that things progress
            at a good and steady rate. I think these first couple steps are my
            secret to success when it comes to finishing books. It deters
            writer’s block because you have a guide to follow, so you always
            know what you’re writing next.
          </p>

          <p>
            Now, of course the actual chapter has a lot more meat to it, but
            I’m just making a first draft off of my list, so it doesn’t need to
            be perfect, and if I’m struggling with what to write to turn my
            list into a chapter, I’m always able to get something down, even if
            it’s complete shit, by at least executing the plot points mentioned.
          </p>

          <p>Then, it’s draft two that does a lot of the cleanup.</p>

          <p>But that’s for a different post!</p>
        </div>

        <footer class="post-footer">
          <a href="/blog" class="back-link">← Back to Blog</a>
        </footer>
      </article>
    </main>

    <footer class="site-footer">
      <div class="container">
        <p>© {new Date().getFullYear()} Ivy Duncan. All rights reserved.</p>
      </div>
    </footer>
  </body>
</html>

<style>
  :global(body) {
    margin: 0;
    background: #fffaf4;
    color: #3f3a35;
    font-family: Georgia, "Times New Roman", serif;
    line-height: 1.8;
  }

  .post-page {
    padding: 5rem 0 7rem;
  }

  .post-container {
    width: min(760px, calc(100% - 3rem));
    margin-inline: auto;
  }

  .post-header {
    margin-bottom: 3rem;
    padding-bottom: 2rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid #c9c2b8;
  }

  .back-link {
    display: inline-block;
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
    color: #765f48;
    font-family: system-ui, sans-serif;
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .back-link:hover {
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 3px;
  }

  .post-date {
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
    color: #765f48;
    font-family: system-ui, sans-serif;
    font-size: 0.85rem;
  }

  .post-header h1 {
    margin: 0 0 1.5rem;
    font-size: clamp(2.5rem, 6vw, 4.5rem);
    line-height: 1.1;
  }

  .post-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.75rem;
  }

  .post-tag {
    color: #765f48;
    font-family: system-ui, sans-serif;
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
    text-decoration: none;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .post-tag:hover {
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 3px;
  }

  .post-content p {
    margin: 0 0 1.5rem;
    font-size: 1.05rem;
  }

  .post-content h2 {
    margin: 3rem 0 1.25rem;
    font-size: clamp(1.7rem, 4vw, 2.25rem);
    line-height: 1.25;
  }

  .post-content ul {
    margin: 0 0 2rem;
    padding-left: 1.5rem;
  }

  .post-content li {
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
    padding-left: 0.25rem;
  }

  .example-document {
    margin: 2rem 0;
    padding: 1.5rem 2rem;
    border-left: 3px solid #765f48;
    background: #f4efe7;
  }

  .example-document .example-title {
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
    font-weight: 700;
  }

  .example-document ul {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .post-footer {
    margin-top: 4rem;
    padding-top: 2rem;
    border-top: 1px solid #c9c2b8;
  }

  .site-footer {
    padding: 2rem 0;
    background: #fffaf4;
  }

  .container {
    width: min(1100px, calc(100% - 3rem));
    margin-inline: auto;
  }

  .site-footer p {
    margin: 0;
    color: #765f48;
    font-family: system-ui, sans-serif;
    font-size: 0.8rem;
  }

  @media (max-width: 700px) {
    .post-page {
      padding: 3rem 0 5rem;
    }

    .post-container {
      width: calc(100% - 2rem);
    }

    .example-document {
      padding: 1.25rem;
    }
  }
</style>